Functions and methods.

What is covered. This notebook walks through def, the method form you will use ninety percent of the time. The function-value form, written as parameter arrow body. The real difference between methods and function values on the java virtual machine, and the eta-expansion that bridges them. Higher-order functions, where functions become arguments and return values. Lambdas, the underscore shorthand, and when each is more readable. Named arguments and default parameters. Multiple parameter lists, and what they enable — better type inference, partial application, and the implicit-context shape you will see in Spark and in notebook eight. Currying and partial application, both with the dot curried helper and with the underscore in argument position. By-name parameters, where you pass an expression rather than its value. Vararg parameters. The function-n family, which is what a function value really is. Local functions, which are methods defined inside other methods. And a brief preview of the inline keyword.

Def — the method form.

The everyday way to declare a piece of executable code in Scala is def. It defines a method, which is a member of a class, a trait, or a singleton object. A top-level def, with no enclosing object, compiles to a static method behind the scenes.

The full shape, with everything optional that is optional, is the keyword def, then a method name, then an optional type parameter list in square brackets, then a parameter list in parentheses, then a colon and a return type, then equals, then the body.

Two things to remember from the start. The return type can be inferred, but for public methods you should write it. The notebook two rule for val applies here even more strongly. The return type is part of the method's contract. And the last expression in the body is the return value. No return keyword needed. You can write return, but in idiomatic Scala you almost never see it — it is mostly a debugging escape hatch.

In code, picture three small methods. Def square, taking x of type integer, returning integer, body x times x. Def hypotenuse, taking two doubles a and b, returning double, body has a val sum of squares followed by a call to math dot square root of that val. Print square of seven, you see forty-nine. Print hypotenuse of three and four, you see five. And a unit method, def log, taking a level and a message, body just prints with a bracketed prefix.

The function-value form — parameter arrow body.

The other way to introduce a function in Scala is as a value. The syntax parentheses x colon integer, arrow, x plus one — produces a function value. An object you can store in a val, pass as an argument, or return from another function.

A function value is a real object on the heap. Its type is one of the function-n traits in the standard library — function zero, function one, function two, all the way up to function twenty-two. The number is the parameter count.

The shape is — parameter list with parentheses, arrow, expression. For functions taking exactly one parameter, the parentheses around the parameter are optional. For functions taking zero parameters, you write empty parentheses arrow expression.

In code, picture this. Val square, of type integer arrow integer, equals parentheses x colon integer, arrow, x times x. Then square of seven is forty-nine. Square dot apply of seven is also forty-nine — the parentheses-call syntax is sugar for dot apply. Then a zero-arg form — val now, of type empty parentheses arrow long, equals empty parentheses arrow system dot current-time-millis. Call now of empty parentheses and you get a timestamp. Then a two-arg form — val add, of type parentheses integer comma integer arrow integer, equals parentheses a b arrow a plus b. The type ascription on the left tells the compiler what type the right-hand side should have. Without the ascription, you would write the types on the parameters themselves.

Methods versus function values — the gap and the bridge.

A def defines a method, which is a member of some enclosing class. A parameter arrow expression introduces a function value, which is a heap object of type function-one or its siblings. On the java virtual machine these are two different things.

A method invocation compiles to an invoke-virtual or invoke-static bytecode instruction — no object allocation. A function value is a real object — calling it goes through its apply method.

This matters because most of the standard library and most Spark application programming interfaces are written to take function values — map, filter, for-each. You cannot pass a def directly to a parameter that expects a function-one of integer to integer. Except that the compiler does it for you. The conversion is called eta-expansion. The compiler wraps the method in a function value automatically when the context expects one.

In Scala 3, eta-expansion happens implicitly anywhere a function type is expected. In Scala 2 you had to write a trailing underscore — square space underscore — to trigger it. You will see that underscore in older code; it is no longer required.

In code, picture a method def double, taking x of integer, returning integer, body x times two. Then a list of one through four, dot map of double. The method double is eta-expanded automatically into a function value. Print the result and you see two, four, six, eight. You can also force the eta-expansion explicitly by ascribing the method to a function type — val double-as-value of integer arrow integer equals double. The opposite direction is also free — wherever a method is expected, a function value works, because function-one defines apply.

Higher-order functions.

A higher-order function is one that either takes a function as a parameter, returns a function, or both. This is the central idea that makes functional programming work — instead of writing one loop for each shape of transform every element, you write one method that takes a transformation as an argument.

The standard library's collection methods are the canonical examples — map, filter, fold-left, reduce, take-while. We will cover those in depth in notebook four. Here the focus is — how you declare a higher-order function, and how you call one.

In code, picture a declaration — def transform-all, taking a list of integer called x-s, and a function f of type integer arrow integer, returning a list of integer, body just x-s dot map of f. Then three different ways to call it. One — pass a previously defined method, like def triple, and the compiler eta-expands. Two — pass an inline lambda, x arrow x times ten. Three — pass the underscore shorthand, underscore plus one hundred. All three return the expected list.

And returning a function from a function. Def adder-factory, taking a base of integer, returning the function integer arrow integer, body returns parentheses x colon integer arrow base plus x. Bind val add-five to adder-factory of five, and val add-ten to adder-factory of ten. Each is now a real function value that adds its captured base to whatever you pass.

Lambdas and the underscore shorthand.

A lambda is just a function value written inline at the call site. The syntax variations all do the same thing — the shortest readable form is the one to use.

Three forms, increasing in compactness. The full form, x-s dot map of parentheses x colon integer arrow x times two. The inferred form, x-s dot map of x arrow x times two — the parameter type is inferred from the expected parameter type of map. And the underscore form, x-s dot map of underscore times two.

The underscore shorthand is the most compact form. Each underscore in the expression stands for a fresh parameter, positionally. Underscore plus underscore is parentheses a b arrow a plus b. It is concise, but it only works when each parameter is used exactly once and in the order it appears. The moment you need to reuse a parameter, or use them out of order, fall back to the named-parameter form.

In code, picture x-s as a list of one through four. The three forms all print two, four, six, eight. Underscore plus underscore on a two-arg reduce — x-s dot reduce of underscore plus underscore — returns the sum, ten. Where underscore breaks down — x-s dot map of underscore arrow underscore — would be interpreted as two underscores, two parameters, not what you meant. The right form is x-s dot map of x arrow x arrow x, which builds the pairs.

Named arguments and default parameters.

Two features that together remove the most common class of bug in any language with positional parameters — passing the right values in the wrong order.

Named arguments. At the call site, you can label any argument with its parameter name. The compiler matches by name, not by position. This makes calls with many parameters readable, and lets you skip optional parameters by name.

Default parameters. In the definition, any parameter can have a default value. Callers that omit it get the default.

Together they enable a style where complex configurations stay readable. Connect of host equals db dot local, port equals fifty four thirty-two, ssl equals true — is unambiguous, even when the same method has eight parameters.

In code, picture a method def connect taking host, port defaulting to fifty four thirty-two, user defaulting to scala, ssl defaulting to false, returning a j-d-b-c connection string. You can call it all positionally — connect of db dot local, fifty four thirty-three, ganesh, true. You can call it with named arguments — connect of host equals db dot local, ssl equals true. You can mix positional and named, as long as positional come first.

Multiple parameter lists — and why they matter.

A def can have more than one parameter list. The syntax just repeats the list — def f, open paren a integer close paren, open paren b integer close paren, returning integer, body a plus b. You call it the same way — f of one of two. At the surface this looks like a cosmetic variation, but it enables three things you cannot get from a single parameter list.

Type inference flows left to right. Scala infers types one parameter list at a time. If the result of the first list constrains what the second list's lambda parameters should be, multiple lists make the inference work. You will see this constantly in higher-order calls — fold-left is declared with two lists exactly for this reason.

Partial application. Apply only the first list, omit the rest, and you get a function value waiting for the missing arguments. We will see this in the next section.

The using parameter list. Scala 3 puts implicit parameters in their own parameter list with the using keyword. Notebook eight covers this in depth. A method like def foo of x integer, then a separate using list, puts the explicit parameter in the first list and the implicit one in the second.

In code, picture fold-left in action. A list of one through four, dot fold-left of zero, then a second parameter list with the combining function — parentheses acc x arrow acc plus x. The seed value zero informs the inference of the accumulator type, and then the lambda parameter types come for free. If fold-left had been declared with one parameter list, you would have had to write the parameter types yourself.

A user-defined example with the same shape — def transform-with, generic over a and b, taking a list of a, then a second list taking a function a arrow b, returning a list of b. At the call site, transform-with of list of one two three, then parentheses underscore times two, the underscore-shorthand lambda has its parameter type inferred from the list element type.

Currying and partial application.

A curried function is one whose parameters live in separate single-element parameter lists. So instead of def add taking a comma b in one list, you write def add of a in one list, of b in a second list. The two are not the same shape at the type level. The curried version has type integer arrow integer arrow integer. The uncurried one has type parentheses integer comma integer arrow integer.

Why anyone bothers. A curried function makes partial application natural. Add of five gives you back a function that adds five to its argument — without writing a lambda yourself.

Scala gives you both directions. Dot curried on a function value returns the curried version. Function dot uncurried of f does the reverse. The underscore in argument position is the manual way to partially apply any method or function — fill in some arguments, leave an underscore for the rest, get back a function.

In code, picture def add-curried in two separate parameter lists. Then val add-five of integer arrow integer equals add-curried of five — the partial application gives you the function. Add-five of three is eight, add-five of ten is fifteen. Picture an uncurried function value, then dot curried on it gives the curried form. Then the underscore-based form on a regular method — def greet taking greeting and name. Val say-hello of string arrow string equals greet of hello comma underscore. Val greet-ganesh of string arrow string equals greet of underscore comma ganesh. Each fills one parameter and leaves the other free.

By-name parameters — passing the expression, not its value.

By default, when you call f of an expression, Scala evaluates the expression before calling f. The value goes in, not the expression.

A by-name parameter flips that. Declared as x colon arrow a — with the arrow but no parameter list to its left — it passes the unevaluated expression. Each time the method body references x, the expression is re-evaluated.

Two real uses. Control-flow application programming interfaces. Building your own if, your own unless, your own retry loop. The body of the construct must not run until the right moment — by-name parameters give you that. And cheap compute only if needed. A logging method's expensive message string should only be built if the log level is enabled. By-name lets you write logger dot debug of the expensive-build call, and have the message-building deferred.

Do not reach for by-name in everyday code. It introduces lazy evaluation in a place callers do not expect, and re-evaluating the same expression every reference can be expensive.

In code, picture two log methods. Log-eager takes a level and a regular message string. Log-lazy takes a level and a by-name message — message colon arrow string. Then a function called expensive that prints a message when it runs and returns a string. Call log-eager with the i-n-f-o level and the result of expensive — expensive runs anyway, even though no log message will be emitted. Call log-lazy with the same arguments — expensive does not run, because the by-name parameter is only evaluated inside the conditional, which does not fire.

Building your own control structure. Def retry, taking an integer times in the first list, and a by-name body in the second list. Body has a loop that tries the body, catches any throwable, retries up to the count, and rethrows the last error. You then use it like a built-in — retry of three, then a brace-block with the attempted code.

Vararg parameters.

A star after a parameter type marks it as a vararg. The caller can pass any number of arguments of that type. Inside the method, the parameter is a sequence of that type.

To pass an existing collection as varargs, append a star to the argument at the call site — f of x-s star. In Scala 2 this was x-s colon underscore star.

In code, picture def join-all, taking a separator string and parts as a vararg of string, returning the parts joined by the separator. Call it as join-all of comma space, then a, b, c — and you get a, b, c. To splat an existing collection, bind val tokens to a list of three strings, then call join-all of pipe space, then tokens star — and you get the joined string.

The function-n family — what a function value really is.

When you write parameter arrow expression, the compiler creates an instance of scala dot function-one of the parameter type and the result type. Function-one is a trait in the standard library; its only abstract method is apply, taking a parameter and returning a result.

There is one function-n trait per arity from function zero, with zero parameters, up to function twenty-two. Beyond twenty-two you have to drop into other shapes — tuples, case classes — but you will rarely hit that limit.

This matters in three places. Reading method signatures — when the Scala docs say a parameter is of type integer arrow integer, they mean function-one of integer and integer. The arrow form is sugar over the trait. Defining function-like classes — any class that defines an apply method can be called with parentheses syntax. My-object of arg desugars to my-object dot apply of arg. This is how list of one two three works — list the companion object has an apply method. And Spark's own internals — Spark's function-one boundary is where serialization happens. When a lambda is shipped to an executor, it is the function value that gets pickled and sent.

In code, picture a val f of type function-one of integer and integer equals parentheses x integer arrow x plus one. Both f dot apply of ten and f of ten return eleven. Then a class greeter with an apply method that takes a name and returns a greeting. Construct it, then call g of ganesh — that desugars to g dot apply of ganesh. This is exactly why list of one two three works — list as a companion object has an apply method, and the parens-call syntax is sugar for dot apply.

Methods inside methods — local functions.

Methods can be defined inside other methods. The inner method is only visible within the enclosing method. Use this when you have a helper that is meaningful only in the context of one outer computation — the alternative is polluting the enclosing class with a private method nobody else should call.

Local methods close over the values in their enclosing scope, so they can reference parameters and local vals of the outer method directly.

In code, picture def format-invoice, taking a list of pairs of name and amount. Inside, def line takes a name and an amount and returns an f-string of the formatted line. Def total has no parameters — it sums the amounts from the captured outer parameter items. Then the body builds the body lines, builds the total footer line, and joins them with newlines. Both inner methods are scoped to format-invoice — they are not visible outside.

Preview — inline def.

Scala 3 adds an inline keyword that turns a method into a compile-time macro. The compiler substitutes the body at the call site instead of generating a real method call.

You will rarely need this in everyday code. It is mostly relevant for library authors writing performance-critical code or compile-time computation. Mentioned here only so the keyword does not surprise you when you encounter it. Notebook seven will use it briefly in the context of opaque types.

Picture inline def square of x integer returning integer equals x times x. Then val n equals square of ten. At compile time, that call site becomes val n equals ten times ten. No method invocation at runtime.

What is next.

You now have functions as first-class values. The difference between methods and function values. And the syntactic features that make Scala's function-rich style pleasant — defaults, named arguments, multiple parameter lists, by-name, varargs.

Notebook four puts these to work on the data shapes you reach for daily — list, vector, set, map, array. And the higher-order operations that operate over them — map, filter, fold, group-by, and the for-yield sugar that desugars into exactly those calls.
